WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.090
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by CuriosityStream, watch over 2,400

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:12.679
documentaries for free for 31 days at curiositystream.com/realengineering.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:17.180
Some crazy things happened during the Cold
War. Dogs were put into orbit, bears were

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.080
fired out of supersonic jets, and humans landed
on the moon. Needless to say, humans were

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.140
going through a rough break-up and were really
trying to find themselves. All of these events

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.960
were centered around one technology that changed
the game for every human on this planet, nuclear

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:33.990
energy.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.770
The bombing of Nagasaki and Hiroshima woke
the world to a nuclear future. Changing the

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:44.370
political and cultural landscape of the world
forever. Infecting the imaginations of people

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.420
all over the world. Spawning monster stories
like Godzilla, in Japan, and heroes like the

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.600
Incredible Hulk and Spiderman in the United
States. People dreamed of a future where electricity

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.440
was free, while simultaneously fearing the
ever-present threat of nuclear annihilation.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.780
These fears and dreams came together to form
perhaps one of the most interesting technologies

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.520
conceived during the Cold War. Nuclear powered
planes.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.890
During World War 2 research and development
of nuclear energy had been focused on its

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.420
weaponization, but with the wars conclusion,
the United States began to seek out ways of

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:24.280
utilizing the power of the atom to fuel their
energy needs. The Atomic Energy Commission

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:30.969
was created in 1946 with the express purpose
of commercialising this new technology, and

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:37.049
just one year later the US Air Force invested
10 million dollars [1] into studying the feasibility

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:43.329
of utilizing this energy to power their long-range
bombers. In an era before in-flight refueling

00:01:43.329 --> 00:01:48.990
and ICBMs had been perfected, the technology
was appealing. With just a small amount of

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:54.200
fuel, a bomber could fly indefinitely. It
would be capable of reaching anywhere in enemy

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.599
territory. An omnipotent threat to any advisory.
Despite the obvious dangers of combining these

00:02:00.599 --> 00:02:04.640
two technologies, the possibilities proved
too tantalizing.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:10.670
From 1948 to 1951 the brunt of the research
centered around a means to transfer the energy

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.080
generated by nuclear fission to propulsion.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.040
Heat energy is gained through nuclear fission.
When uranium is bombarded with a neutron it

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.239
absorbs that neutron into its nucleus, which
causes severe vibrations ripping the atom

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:30.700
apart, producing heat, additional neutrons
and new lighter atoms. But the sum of products

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.489
of that split are lighter than the original
atom.[2] Experimental proof of Einstein's

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:41.290
ground breaking 1905 paper teaching the world
of the energy and mass relationship through

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:47.280
the equation of E equals m c squared. The
energy released by a single uranium fission

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:53.800
reaction like this is tiny, at 200 million
electron volts [3], but crucially uranium

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:59.430
produces additional neutrons when it splits
allowing for a chain reaction to occur. So,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:04.280
for very little input energy we can get a
tremendous amount of kinetic energy as an

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:10.079
output in the form of heat. When controlled
this reaction can give us energy to heat water

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:15.900
and power our steam turbines when uncontrolled
this reaction gives us the atomic bomb.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:21.080
Experiments began here in the Idaho National
Engineering and Environment Laboratory. Dubbed

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:27.019
the HTRE, standing for Heat Transfer Reactor
Experiment, these engines sought to find the

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:31.510
most efficient solution for transforming this
thermal energy into thrust. [4]

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:39.010
They eventually came to the HTRE-3. Consisting
of 2 modified general electric J47 engines,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:44.409
that would perform both propulsion and cooling
functions. Air would be ducted from the low-pressure

00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:49.379
compressor through the reactor core, where
it would gain heat and expand, this air would

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:54.379
then pass through the high-pressure turbine
and exhaust to provide power and thrust.

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:59.629
As the air was needed for cooling, the engine
had to be started using traditional fuel sources,

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:04.769
allowing the air to pass through the cool
reactor. Once sufficient airflow was achieved

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:09.240
the reactor could then be brought up to power.
The engine contained a temperature control

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.900
thermocouple [5], which fed data to a control
module that would automatically close the

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:19.120
chemical fuel valve as the heat of the nuclear
reactor began to be added until the valve

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.000
was completely closed.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.810
The HTRE-3 was successfully run multiple times,
but there were still several problems to be

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:31.540
solved. Perhaps chief among these was the
energy transfer method’s propensity for

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.090
spewing radioactive air out of its exhaust.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:40.560
This was an open or direct cycle configuration,
meaning the air is directly used to cool the

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.760
reactor core. [6] This was a simpler set-up,
requiring no additional pumps within the nuclear

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.950
reactor, that the program managers at General
Electric preferred, but resulted in air passing

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:56.140
through the highly radioactive core and thus
being contaminated, and subsequently exhausted

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.470
to atmosphere.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:01.920
Obviously not an ideal situation, and this
program actually spurred the creation of the

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:09.730
very first molten salt nuclear reactor through
the ARE, or Aircraft Reactor Experiment.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:15.560
This was instead a closed cycle system, where
a molten uranium tetrafluoride salt is used

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.910
a fuel, while a secondary closed loop containing
molten salt with no uranium was used as a

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:27.340
coolant. This coolant would then pass through
a liquid-to-air heat exchanger to power the

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:32.720
turbine. This would result in radically reduced
radioactivity in the exhaust, but required

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.120
more plumbing to circulate the liquids in
the two inner closed loops, and resulted in

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:43.120
a lower efficiency as we are introducing an
additional heat transfer step that allows

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.470
more heat to the be lost to the plumbing and
environment.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:52.130
This method was never tested with a jet engine,
but this is the earliest ancestor of the thorium

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:57.020
reactors which are easily the most requested
topic on this channel, as a result of their

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:02.210
potential to provide cheap and safe nuclear
energy, but research and funding for this

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.260
technology was gradually dropped.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:09.330
Had the program developed further, these molten
salt reactors likely would have gone on to

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.430
power any eventually bomber, had the power
to weight issues been overcome.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:18.861
These power to weight issues were one of the
primary roads blocks facing designers. While

00:06:18.861 --> 00:06:24.240
nuclear energy can provide extremely long
lasting energy, its power output, the energy

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:30.840
provided per unit time, is not infinite. Nuclear
reactors like this have maximum power settings,

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.370
limited by the heat the cooling system can
transport away before it can begin to melt

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:40.460
and damage the reactor and it’s control
mechanisms. This made it difficult to design

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.720
a reactor capable of providing enough energy
to power jet engines with enough thrust to

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:49.270
get the gargantuan weight of the reactor off
the ground.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:56.300
The HTRE-3 is estimated to have weighed 45
metric tonnes and could produce up to 35 megawatts

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:01.890
of thermal output. Far too large and heavy,
and short of the 50 megawatts of thermal output

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.550
targeted for a flight worthy power source.
[2]

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:10.680
The HTRE-3 did include removable radiation
shielding, consisting of a stainless steel

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.270
shell with a lead core surrounded by water
[R1-4], but further testing was needed to

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.430
design and assess shielding for an aircraft.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:26.440
To do this Convair modified a B-36, renaming
it the NB-36H Crusader. The B-36 was the only

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:32.330
aircraft in the United State’s arsenal capable
of taking off with a massive nuclear reactor,

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:37.930
and it’s associated shielding and coolant
systems. Fitting it with a small 1 megawatt

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:43.710
reactor, dubbed the ASTR, or aircraft shield
test reactor, which was lifted from a shielded

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:48.580
underground vault and mounted in the B-36s
bomb bay moments before take-off. [1][10]

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:53.840
A plane could never carry the amount of lead
needed to shield every facet of a reactor,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.920
so this plane would test shadow shielding,
which would primarily shield the crew and

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.590
instruments in the cockpit. Shielding for
the reactor was achieved with water tanks

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:07.190
which could be filled or drained to vary the
shielding and allow the nuclear engineers

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:13.180
on board to assess the minimum volume of water
needed to protect the planes crew and instruments,

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:18.540
with a 5 tonne 13-centimetre lead shield mounted
between the reactor and the crew compartment.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.580
On top of this, the crew compartment was an
11 tonne lead and rubber shielded removable

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:28.330
section. The leaded glass windows were a foot
thick, and a closed circuit tv system was

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:30.350
used to monitor the reactor.

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:34.729
The plane was further modified with air scoops
to funnel air to the coolant system that would

00:08:34.729 --> 00:08:40.390
trade heat with the internal closed loop water
coolant, and then exhaust the heat to atmosphere.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:46.870
The Crusader made its maiden flight on September
17th 1955. The reactor provided no power to

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:51.980
the engines, but the plane would make a total
of 47 flights, which occured only over remote

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:57.630
land far from human populations. The plane
was escorted at all times by a B-50, which

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:02.760
contained sensors to measure any air scattered
radiation emitted from the reactor and air

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:07.670
coolant system. It also contained a team of
marines ready to parachute and secure a crash

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.530
location if the need arose.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:15.440
At this point, the United States was at the
forefront of aircraft nuclear propulsion technology

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:20.490
and likely could have developed the technology
far enough to produce an nuclear-powered plane.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:24.120
But, for the best, it never came to fruition.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:31.620
On November 18th, 1958 the HTRE-3 engine suffered
a meltdown when temperature sensors malfunctioned,

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.190
recording a lower temperature and withdrawing
control rods. [7]

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:39.630
This may have been the impetus to shake sense
into the US Government, to remind them that

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:44.860
a mobile nuclear meltdown was simply not something
they wanted to contend with. On top of all

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:49.580
this, with the advancement of aerial refueling
and intercontinental ballistic missiles, the

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.420
technology was made completely redundant.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:58.570
President Kennedy ultimately cancelled the
program in 1961, just 1 month into his presidency.

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:03.950
Putting an end to the insane idea. This hysteria
and fear of aerial bombing has made humans

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:08.260
do some astounding evil and stupid things.
If you would like to learn more about this

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:13.490
gradual descent into madness that the advent
of aerial bombing fostered, I highly recommend

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:17.880
watching this documentary titled the Bombing
War: From Guernica to Hiroshima. It takes

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:22.480
you from the first small grenade dropped from
a plane in World War 1 all the way to the

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:26.840
monstrous and unnecessary bombing campaigns
that took place in Europe and Asia in World

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:31.750
War 2. It’s a fascinating insight into the
minds of the civilians that lived through

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:36.070
those times and the madness that gripped the
military leaders that approved them.

